7 544. Bye- laws to be published. The bye-laws made under Section 541 shall be published in the Official Gazette." 
7. In the above statutory context, it is the case of the petitioners that the impugned Bye-law namely the Nagar Nigam, Bareilly (Vigyapano Ka Viniyaman Aur Niyantran Evam Anugyapti Shulk Nirdharan Aur Vasuli) Upvidhi 2020 (hereinafter referred to as the Bye-law) has been notified wholly in violation of the mandatory provisions of the Act, noted above. Thus, it has been submitted:-
8. No notice was published in the Official Gazette. Relying on the five judge bench decision of the Supreme Court in Commissioner of Income Tax, Mumbai v. Anjum M.H. Ghaswala and others,
2002(1) SCC 633, it has been submitted, when a statute requires a thing to be done in a particular manner, it may either be done in that way or not at all. Objections were never invited in terms of Section 543(a) of the Act. Thus, neither any Gazette publication was made nor the newspaper/Bulletin publication made, specified the date after which the Bye-law was to be taken into consideration by the Municipal Corporation. As to lack of publication in the Official Gazette,
reliance has been placed on State of M.P. and another v. Ram Ragubir Prasad Agarwal and others,
AIR 1979 SC 888. In absence of publication in the Official Gazette, the entire action taken by the respondent is invalid. As a fact no objections were invited by the Municipal Corporation to the impugned Bye-law, in the manner prescribed. Therefore, the Bye-law may not have been notified and enforced without prior approval of the Municipal Corporation. That action has not been performed by the collective body and/ or the body incorporate. Reference has been made to pleadings made in paragraph nos. 8, 9, 11 and 15 of the Writ Petition. They read as below:-
"8. That, the proposed byelaws were published on the official website of the Respondent 2 and was published in the newspaper of Dainik Jagaran and Amar Ujala on 16.02.2021 calling for objections and suggestions. Copy of the advertisement dated 16.02.2021 in Dainik Jagaran and Amar Ujala newspapers are attached as ANNEXURE-1.
9. That, however, as required under law, No gazette publication of the Notice was made by the Respondent 2 and hence the entire proceeding is vitiated in law. 
11. That, thereafter, under law, for the purposes of deciding objections received by corporation, section 543(d) provided fro hearing and decision on such matters.
15. That, wholly without any authority of law, the objections taken by Petitioners was decided by Respondent 3 on 16.08.2021 and not by the Corporation, as mandated under law. Copy of the objections so decided on 16.08.2021 is attached as Annexure-5." 
9. Submission is, the objections invited by the Municipal Corporation Bareilly have not been dealt with and decided by that body. In fact the Municipal Commissioner has merely accepted the Office Note submitted by a three-member Committee comprising the Revenue Inspector, the Assistant Aar Publicity And 6 Others vs State Of U.P. And Another on 22 July, 2024 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/65903618/
8 Engineer and Incharge (Advertisement). Reply to the above pleadings is contained in paragraph Nos.5, 6 and 7 of the Counter Affidavit of the respondent Corporation filed by Shri Jatin Kuldeep Singh, Prabhari Law, Nagar Nigam, Bareilly. They read as below:-
"5. That the contents of paragraph no.5,6,7 and 8 of the writ petition need no comment as matter of fact. 
6. That the contents of paragraph no.9 of the writ petition are not correct and hence denied, in reply it is stated here that the concerned person to whom the authority has been delegated to issue the gazette notice have been done by the same authority. 
7. That the contents of paragraph no.10,11,12,13,14 and 15 of the writ petition are not correct and hence denied, in reply that the objection of the petitioner was placed before the Nigam and the noting was prepared against the objections, in a chart form vis-a vis was prepared by the Nigam in which column no.1 their objections were noted and the next column no.2 the onion of the Nigam was opined evident from the signature appended by the Nigam official dated 23.06.2021 dated 22.07.2021 calling for personal hearing in pursuance to which no appearance was made by them on the date fixed i.e. 26.07.2021 after which again letter dated 11.08.2021 was issued vide which the hearing took place on 16.08.2021 and taking note of their objections as well as personal hearing, further noting upon the said objections were prepared by the Nigam on the same date 16.08.2021 which thereafter vide amended draft dated 24.08.2021 was presented before the Board for its decision and approval on 28.08.2021 after which only the same was finalized and Published in the gazette notification dated 01.01.2022. A copy of the chart dated 23.06.2021 along with noting dated 16.08.2021 as well as the attendance sheet are all hereby marked collectively as Annexure No. CA-1 to this counter affidavit. 
10. Reliance has been placed on a decision of the co-ordinate bench of this Court in U.P. Advertisers Association Vs. State of U.P and 2 Others 2017 (5) ADJ 788.
11. Shri Sanjeev Singh and Shri Shyam Mani Shukla, learned counsel appearing for the Municipal Corporation would submit, though Gazette publication was not made, at the same time newspaper publication was made on 15/16.02.2021, inviting objections to the draft Bye-law. Not only such publication was made but the petitioners filed their written objections as is their admitted case. 
Therefore, no legal injury has been caused to the petitioners owing to non-publication of notice inviting objection in the official gazette. 
12. Second, it has been submitted, the objections filed by the petitioners were dealt with and decided as is clear from a bare perusal of Annexure no.1 to the Counter Affidavit to the writ petition filed by M/s S.S. Graphics. 
13. Since the Municipal Board could not have dealt with individual objections, the same were dealt with by a three-member Committee comprising of the Tax Inspector, Assistant Engineer, Nagar Aar Publicity And 6 Others vs State Of U.P. And Another on 22 July, 2024 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/65903618/
9 Nigam Bareilly and Incharge Advertisement, Nagar Nigam Bareilly, vide their report dated 23.06.2021. Those were placed before the Municipal Commissioner who accorded his approval, on 16.08.2021. However, the Bye-law was not enforced pursuant to that decision. Rather, the matter was placed before the Municipal Board in its meeting on 28.08.2021 wherein it was approved by means of Supplementary Agenda, after recording amendment in terms of the discussion of the Board. 
14. Having heard learned counsel for the parties and having perused the record, in the first place, it is to be noted that the power to frame and enforced Bye-law is a pure legislative function. There is no doubt and there can be admitted no possibility of a doubt in that regard. In the context of that delegated law making function, the State legislature has prescribed a strict requirement on the Municipal Corporation to first invite objections to the proposed Bye-law and second it has prescribed the manner in which such objections may be invited and considered, before the Bye-law may be enforced. 
15. To the first part, there is no reservation expressed by the Municipal Corporation. Thus it admits that the petitioner and others had a perfect right to object to the proposed Bye-law. As to the manner in which such objections may have been invited, Section 543(a) is crystal clear. It requires publication of a notice, both in the Official Gazette and in the Bulletin of the Corporation, if any. 
Thus, the principal requirement enforced by the Act is to publish the notice inviting objections in the Official Gazette. Additionally, but not alternatively, if any Bulletin were being published by the Municipal Corporation, it would remain obligated to publish the same in that Bulletin, as well. Here,
it is an admitted case that Bareilly Municipal Corporation does not publish a Bulletin of its own. 
Therefore, it may have been open to that Municipal Corporation to publish the notice in a newspaper, as well. However, that compliance would not obviate the pre-existing essential requirement to publish that notice in the Official Gazette. That first compliance has not been made. 
In the context of law making function delegated on the Municipal Corporation, Bareilly, that is incurable defect. A law whether made by a principal legislature or it's delegate must always arise/enacted and be enforced, strictly in accordance with the fundamental law of the land-the Constitution of India. Additionally, a delegate must also strictly conform to the tenets of the principal legislation, under which it may act. Unless that act is performed by the delegate strictly as ordained by the principal legislation, it would remain invalid. A statutory law once enforced creates the frame work for conduct of human affairs and transactions. By its very nature, it applies generally to all members of a society, present and living and those who may be born later. It is not an executive or judicial function that may primarily seek to address particular facts. On the contrary, a legislative act transcends individual facts and seeks to define the conduct of humans in a situation. 
Therefore, it may arise strictly in the manner prescribed. 
16. Second, the notice published in the newspaper Dainik Jagran on 16.2.2021 reads as below :
"     ,      173/  . / .. . /2020 - 21  15.02.21                           1559     401,438,451,452   541(20)(41)  (49)            
                -2018      ,
