10      /          /        
15             nagarnigambareilly.com                              



       ,  " 
17. The said notice does not specify any date after which Municipal Corporation Bareilly intended to take up the proposed Bye-law i.e. to enforce it. Therefore, even the Bulletin/newspaper publication remained defective and incomplete. 
18. In the context of legislative function being exercised by the Municipal Corporation Bareilly, we recognise that even in the realm of administrative law, a rule exists-a function statutorily prescribed to be performed in a particular way, may either be performed in that way or not at all. We also recognise the importance of the publication made in the Official Gazettee as that is the first and irrebuttable evidence of public notification or publication made. At the same time, in the facts of the present case, we find that the function giving rise to the lis between the parties, is legislative. For exercise of that legislative function, the principal legislature has enacted Sections 541, 542 and 543(a) and laid a strict procedure to be applied before a valid delegated legislation may arise. Since publication of the prior notice is required to be made in that Official Gazettee and also in the Bulletin of the Corporation, if any, no bye-law may have come into force without strict prior compliance of this procedure. In absence of publication made in that Official Gazette, that requirement remained unfulfilled. No benefit may be drawn by the Municipal Corporation Bareilly on the strength of publication made in the newspaper inasmuch as that publication may only be equated with a publication made in a Bulletin. That may not stand on a better footing than an additional requirement and not an alternate to the mandatory requirement of the law, namely publication of the prior notice in the Official Gazette. 
19. Then, the notice if any, to be published in the newspaper, was also deficient inasmuch as it did not fulfil the strict requirement of Section 543(a). It did not specify the date after which Municipal Corporation Bareilly proposed to take into consideration the Bye-law. Merely giving 15 days' time to object, may remain an indirect suggestion to the noticee that the Bye-law may not be taken up till expiry of 15 days from the publication. However, it is not the requirement of the Act to give notice of time during which the proposed Bye-law may not be enforced. Rather, the requirement is to enable the noticee to also object to the time when the Bye-law proposed, may be enforced. 
20. Third, as to the consideration made to the objection, while we may agree with learned counsel for the respondents that three-member Committee considered the objection and submitted its report to the Municipal Commissioner and that the Municipal Corporation agreed with the view of the three-member Committee, at the same time, we are unable to accept the further contention that such report of the three-member Committee was ever approved by the Municipal Corporation Bareilly. In the first place, the notice for the meeting dated 28.8.2021 annexed as Annexure-6 to the Aar Publicity And 6 Others vs State Of U.P. And Another on 22 July, 2024 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/65903618/ 
