22. Thus, it is not shown to us that there was any Supplementary Agenda circulated amongst the members of the Municipal Corporation of the Bareilly, informing them about the consideration to be made to the proposed Bye-law or the objections received thereto. 
23. Second, no material-either the objections or the report submitted by the three-member Committee (as approved by the Municipal Commissioner) has been referred to in those minutes. No intrinsic or extrinsic evidence has been shown to exist as may allow us to entertain any possibility that the proposed Bye-law and the objections filed thereto were either placed before or were considered by the collective body i.e. Municipal Corporation, at its meeting on 28.8.2021.
24. Then, in U.P. Advertisers Association (supra), a coordinate bench of this Court had specifically held that no liability to pay tax etc. may be imposed except in accordance with the valid law framed. 
Though this is not a case of involving levy of tax at the same time, for reasons noted above, we are not inclined to take any other view in the present facts. 
25. The aforesaid writ petitions must succeed. Accordingly, they are allowed. The Bye-law is declared to be unconstitutional to the extent valid procedure has not been complied to invite and deal with the objection thereto. Also, the same has been duly approved by the Municipal Corporation, Bareilly. However, we leave it open to the Municipal Corporation to act in accordance with law from the stage of inviting fresh objections. 
26. Consequentially, the demand orders are quashed. 
27. No order as to costs. 
